Vulvovaginal Candidiasis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Thick white clumpy discharge, Intense vulvar pruritus, Vulvovaginal erythema

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CIA-050
Reason for Visit: Intense, agonizing vaginal burning and itching preventing sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Patient triaged via telephone reporting acute, severe vulvovaginal pruritus and burning that has reached an intolerable threshold, causing significant distress and preventing sleep. The patient describes a "raw" sensation and severe external dysuria. An urgent home assessment is required to perform a focused examination, rule out more complex infections (e.g., Trichomoniasis or severe Bacterial Vaginosis), and provide immediate antifungal relief. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of prolonged acute suffering and secondary excoriation from scratching, satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The itching and burning are so intense I can't sit still or sleep. It feels like my skin is on fire.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 48 hours ago and have escalated rapidly. The patient reports an intense vulvar pruritus and burning sensation. Notes a thick, white, and clumpy "cottage-cheese-like" vaginal discharge. Experiences significant dyspareunia and external dysuria when urine contacts the irritated skin. Patient notes a preceding 7-day course of broad-spectrum antibiotics for a sinus infection two weeks ago. Denies a foul or fishy odor. Symptoms have exacerbated just prior to her expected menses.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute distress.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 mmHg | HR: 82 bpm | Temp: 36.8 °C | RR: 14 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Appears restless and visibly distressed by localized symptoms.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A positive vaginal wet mount revealing budding yeast and pseudohyphae would assist in confirming the diagnosis, but clinical features are pathognomonic.
Physical Exam: Pelvic examination reveals significant erythema and edema of the vulva and vaginal mucosa. Copious, thick, white, clumpy discharge is visible. There is no evidence of a malodorous discharge or "clue cells" on visual inspection. The cervix appears entirely normal and healthy without discharge or friability. No inguinal lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Safe residential environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Recently finished a course of Amoxicillin.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Vulvovaginal Candidiasis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Bacterial Vaginosis - Ruled out by the absence of a characteristic fishy odor and the presence of clumpy (rather than thin/grey) discharge.
Trichomoniasis - Unlikely given the lack of a frothy, green-yellow discharge and the normal appearance of the cervix (no "strawberry cervix").
Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out by the presence of the characteristic white, curd-like discharge, although localized skin irritation is present secondary to the yeast.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient's acute, severe pruritus and localized burning caused a high degree of distress, necessitating an urgent evaluation. On-site physical examination confirmed a classic, severe presentation of vulvovaginal candidiasis (thrush), likely secondary to recent antibiotic use. By definitively diagnosing the fungal etiology and providing immediate topical and oral antifungal therapy, the acute inflammatory response was addressed, allowing the patient to achieve immediate relief and rest. This clinical stabilization could not safely be deferred, fulfilling MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a single dose of Fluconazole 150mg orally stat. Recommended Clotrimazole 1% cream to be applied to the vulva twice daily for 7 days for symptomatic relief.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically prevent the yeast from multiplying further, strictly wear only loose-fitting, 100% cotton underwear and avoid all scented soaps or "feminine hygiene" sprays. Yeast thrives in warm, moist, and chemically irritated environments; removing synthetic fabrics and perfumes allows the skin to breathe and restores the natural pH balance, making it impossible for the yeast to survive.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 1 week if symptoms do not fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Seek medical review if you develop a high fever, severe pelvic pain, or if the redness begins to spread rapidly beyond the groin area.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; recurrent thrush (more than 4 episodes per year) requires further investigation into underlying causes.
Clinical Course: Pruritus and burning should improve significantly within 24-48 hours of taking the oral tablet. Complete resolution of discharge and redness takes 5-7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate review if you notice a new, foul-smelling discharge or if the external skin develops painful blisters.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The antibiotics you took killed the "good" bacteria that usually keep your natural yeast in check. The yeast has overgrown, causing the clumpy discharge and chemical-like burn on your skin. The tablet kills the yeast from the inside out.
Actionable Steps: Avoid sexual intercourse until the infection has completely cleared to prevent further mechanical irritation to the raw tissue.

